{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB04819 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Methapyrilene is implicated in the pathogenesis of Allergic Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Methapyrilene may act as a mediator in the development of allergic rhinitis by influencing vasomotor rhinitis, a condition characterized by inflammation of the nasal mucosa. This is supported by the indication of Methapyrilene with Vasomotor Rhinitis, which in turn is linked to medications like Isoflupredone Acetate and Mizolastine, both of which are known to treat allergic rhinitis. The final score of 0.4629 in the first path and 0.4495 in the second and third paths indicates a consistent but somewhat lower confidence in the direct link between Methapyrilene and Allergic Rhinitis, likely due to the indirect nature of the pathways. The recurring mechanism involves the modulation of nasal inflammation through vasomotor effects, which may be a key pathway in the repurposing of Methapyrilene for allergic rhinitis.",
